## Why This Is Asked

Evaluate ability to implement host integrity controls with minimal downtime using native RH tooling, idempotence, and rollback.

## Key Concepts

- rpm -V usage for binary integrity
- systemd service and timer
- Baseline diffing and logging
- Alerting and rollback strategy
- Reinstall path from diffs

## Code Example

```javascript
const { execSync } = require('child_process');
const fs = require('fs');
const baseline = '/var/log/integrity/baseline.txt';
const current = execSync('rpm -V -a').toString();
const base = fs.readFileSync(baseline, 'utf8');
const nl = String.fromCharCode(10);
const diff = current.split(/\\r?\\n/).filter(l => !base.includes(l)).join(nl);
if (diff) {
  fs.appendFileSync('/var/log/sys_integrity.log', diff + nl);
  console.log('ALERT');
} else {
  console.log('OK');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for multi-host environments and centralize reporting?
- How would you minimize false positives around legitimate upgrades?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses end-to-end TLS termination, dynamic cert provisioning, and zero-downtime reload in a Podman-based RH9 setup. Also tests firewall (nftables), SELinux, and service upgrade discipline.

## Key Concepts
- Podman, nginx as TLS terminator, Vault integration, in-container watcher, systemd upgrades, nftables, SELinux, SNI routing

## Code Example
```nginx
# tls_preread for SNI, per-tenant certs loaded from /etc/nginx/certs
stream {
  map $ssl_preread_server_name $tenant {
    tenantA backendA;
    tenantB backendB;
    tenantC backendC;
  }
  server {
    listen 8443;
    ssl_preread on;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Vault outage and certificate revocation?
- How to validate zero-downtime during upgrades?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical firewall configuration skills on RHEL 9, requiring precise rule creation, persistence, and verification—essential for enterprise security administration.

## Key Concepts
- nftables ruleset syntax and packet flow
- Rule persistence via /etc/nftables.conf
- Service management with systemctl
- Connectivity testing from allowed vs. disallowed sources
- Verification using nft list ruleset

## Code Example
```bash
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset
table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
    iifname "lo" accept
    ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 accept
  }
  chain forward {
    type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
  }
  chain output {
    type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
  }
}
```

## Implementation Steps
```bash
# Install nftables
dnf install -y nftables

# Create ruleset
cat > /etc/nftables.conf << 'EOF'
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
flush ruleset
table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0; policy drop;
    iifname "lo" accept
    ip saddr 192.168.1.0/24 tcp dport 22 accept
  }
  chain forward {
    type filter hook forward priority 0; policy drop;
  }
  chain output {
    type filter hook output priority 0; policy accept;
  }
}
EOF

# Enable and start services
systemctl enable nftables
systemctl start nftables
systemctl status nftables

# Verify rules
nft list ruleset

# Test connectivity (from allowed host)
ssh user@server-ip

# Test connectivity (from disallowed host)
ssh user@server-ip  # Should timeout/refuse
```
